WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:12.150
we take and the responsibilities that we bear
are of intense public interest. The faith

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:17.600
and confidence that the public of England
have in the National Health Service befits

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:23.380
that we should consider issues affecting its
funding, its future and its transformation

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:27.070
in a public setting.

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:32.009
Two caveats to that. The first is that we
will conducting the principal business of

00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:37.650
this meeting in this public setting but always
there are confidential items that we need

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:43.900
to take in a private meeting at the close
of this the public meeting. Secondly, to stress

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:48.570
always of course that although this is the
high point of the conduct of business by the

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:54.550
Board we do a lot in other forums. We do a
lot in committees. There will at the end of

00:00:54.550 --> 00:01:01.010
today's formal agenda be some reports from
committees and I would highlight particularly

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:05.400
a new committee, the Commissioning Committee,
that met for the first time yesterday, under

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:10.000
the chairmanship of David Roberts. He will
be making a report to us today on the business

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:11.860
that they were able to transact.

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:18.860
A couple of days ago we had a meeting of the
Task &amp; Finish Group on congenital heart disease.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:24.190
We will be bringing to the July Board a paper
that has been worked up over an extensive

00:01:24.190 --> 00:01:28.170
process of public consultation.

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:35.170
Yesterday the Board met with the NHS Citizens
and participated in a session with them here

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:40.190
in Leeds, yesterday afternoon, which I think
I can say on behalf of all of the Board we

00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:46.460
found exceptionally interesting. It helps
us to get away from some of the high level

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:52.799
issues of funding and trying to drive change
and to actually engage with, not just listen

00:01:52.799 --> 00:01:57.500
to but engage with people who are having a
daily experience of the services that the

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:03.510
NHS provides across the country. To work with
them around issues of improving service and

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:08.700
co-design and actually working through with
them a number of issues that they had raised

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:14.939
with us last September at our AGM. We were
able to report on what progress we had made,

00:02:14.939 --> 00:02:18.159
where there had been success, where there
was disappointment and what our commitments

00:02:18.159 --> 00:02:21.909
were for future development.

00:02:21.909 --> 00:02:25.730
We also spent some time yesterday afternoon
in what we call a Development Session, which

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:32.730
is the Board in informal session thinking
about the longer-term strategic issues and

00:02:33.409 --> 00:02:39.939
particularly in the really tight funding settlement
that we have got for 2015/16, what will be

00:02:39.939 --> 00:02:43.709
the priorities and how we will address them.
We will come back to some of that in the course

00:02:43.709 --> 00:02:49.439
of today's meeting when we review the principles
that will underline our business plan for

00:02:49.439 --> 00:02:52.769
next year.

00:02:52.769 --> 00:02:59.769
That I hope gives a contextual picture of
the way in which the Board operates. I have

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:04.989
always been very keen to ensure that this
was not a Board which at a formal meeting

00:03:04.989 --> 00:03:10.950
received proposals that have been worked out
by the Executive Team, subjected them to cold

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:16.269
scrutiny and then dispatched them. This is
a Board that works together as a team across

00:03:16.269 --> 00:03:19.659
the whole business.

00:03:19.659 --> 00:03:26.659
Just one final issue which is to say that
we have been experimenting for the last couple

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:33.309
of years with producing our Board papers in
electronic format so you will see Board members

00:03:33.309 --> 00:03:39.739
referring to iPads. These contain the full
Board papers. They are not being used for

00:03:39.739 --> 00:03:46.739
Angry Birds or any other illicit function.
When Board members' eyes are fixed on their

00:03:47.529 --> 00:03:54.529
screens they are totally focused on the business
of the Board. I hope that is clear.

00:03:54.939 --> 00:04:01.449
Let me please then declare the meeting formally
open and move into the formal business to

00:04:01.449 --> 00:04:05.949
say that there is no fire alarm scheduled
in this building this morning. If the alarm

00:04:05.949 --> 00:04:12.749
does sound we will evacuate the building and
we will be led by our team. Secondly, I am

00:04:12.749 --> 00:04:18.010
going to invite Board members to declare any
interests that they may have in specific items

00:04:18.010 --> 00:04:20.070
of business on the agenda.

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:27.070
I need to declare in relation to Item 7, which
is genomics, that I am a Board Director of

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:34.629
Genomics England Ltd. Two things about that.
First of all, that is a company wholly owned

00:04:34.629 --> 00:04:40.860
by the Secretary of State for Health to carry
forward the 100,000 Genomes Project. Secondly,

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:45.919
I am not NHS England's representative on the
Board. There is a Board Director. My responsibility,

00:04:45.919 --> 00:04:51.250
my fiduciary duties are to the Board of Genomics
England but my presence on the Board is, I

00:04:51.250 --> 00:04:57.379
think, a valuable way of balancing the quite
complex partnership that we, NHS England,

00:04:57.379 --> 00:04:59.430
have with Genomics England.

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:03.140
Are there other declarations of interest?
Moira?

00:05:03.140 --> 00:05:07.159
&gt;&gt; Moira Gibb: I should just re-state that
I am the Chair of Skills for Care and related

00:05:07.159 --> 00:05:09.750
to the Adults with Care paper.

00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:15.900
&gt;&gt; Malcolm Grant: Thank you. There are no
other declarations of interest. I have no

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:20.810
apologies for absence. This is a full house
and indeed we will be complemented during

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:27.810
the morning by David Pearson, who is the President
of ADASS, and Carolyn Downs, Chief Executive

00:05:28.199 --> 00:05:35.199
of the Local Government Association, who will
speak to the paper on social care.

00:05:35.349 --> 00:05:41.669
The handling of business, we will probably
take the two items of committee reports that

00:05:41.669 --> 00:05:46.530
are formally on the agenda as starred items
unless in the course of the meeting anybody

00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:50.800
indicates that they would like to open those
items. However, the report from the Commissioning

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:57.800
Committee I will take as a full item of business.

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:08.960
I would like to invite the Board to signify
their approval of the minutes. Thank you.

00:06:09.129 --> 00:06:16.129
I have no matters arising that are not otherwise
on the agenda. Anybody else? No, thank you.

00:06:17.849 --> 00:06:24.490
&gt;&gt; Simon Stevens: Thank you, Malcolm. I would
first of all like to thank all of the staff

00:06:24.490 --> 00:06:29.930
of the National Health Service who were involved
in NHS Change Day, two weeks ago, which was

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:36.930
a fantastic mobilising of energy and optimism,
dare I say it, on the part of tens of thousands

00:06:37.719 --> 00:06:42.860
of nurses, junior doctors, therapists, porters,
other staff across the Health Service, all

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:48.599
of whom were identifying things that they
could see they wanted to change in their part

00:06:48.599 --> 00:06:53.289
of the Health Service, making commitments
to do so and taking action on the back of

00:06:53.289 --> 00:06:59.249
that. I think that is a good corrective to
the understandable pressures that everybody

00:06:59.249 --> 00:07:06.020
is under and a sense of real ability to re-shape
services, which is obviously what we are going

00:07:06.020 --> 00:07:08.300
to need over the period ahead.

00:07:08.300 --> 00:07:15.300
I spent the day with a nurse called Andy Tysoe
from Countess of Chester Hospital. He gave

00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:20.819
me my Dementia Friends training. Then I spent
part of the afternoon with a group of people

00:07:20.819 --> 00:07:26.629
with learning disabilities and their families,
and met a young woman who had been living

00:07:26.629 --> 00:07:33.379
in institutional care for years. She came
to show me her video of her sailing in the

00:07:33.379 --> 00:07:38.819
Solent as a result of having taken control
of personal budget for those services.

00:07:38.819 --> 00:07:45.639
I met another woman called Jean Riley, from
Yorkshire who described the massive impact

00:07:45.639 --> 00:07:49.800
on the care of her daughter that being able
to take more control of the health and social

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:54.889
care that she had. In fact I was so impressed
with what Jean Riley had to say that I took

00:07:54.889 --> 00:07:59.639
her with me, the very next day, to a speech
I was giving to the Royal College of Physicians

00:07:59.639 --> 00:08:05.569
in Harrogate and we did a double act together.
She explained precisely how care can change

00:08:05.569 --> 00:08:10.889
on the back of the frankly revolution in the
balance of power that has occurred. Those

00:08:10.889 --> 00:08:15.659
are great sources of optimism. Huge thanks
to all of the frontline staff involved in

00:08:15.659 --> 00:08:20.729
NHS Change Day.

00:08:20.729 --> 00:08:25.300
Changing gear a little, as we think about
the current position in the National Health

00:08:25.300 --> 00:08:31.599
Service, later on the agenda we will have
the report from Barbara describing the current

00:08:31.599 --> 00:08:38.500
pressures performance. We are obviously at
a point in the annual cycle where providers

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:45.030
and commissioners, CCGs, NHS England, working
out what is the appropriate way of squaring

00:08:45.030 --> 00:08:50.690
the circle between funding and investment
and activity and improvement for the year

00:08:50.690 --> 00:08:56.520
ahead. No doubt that this is going to be a
complex task over the next four to six weeks;

00:08:56.520 --> 00:09:02.330
everybody using best endeavours to do the
right thing for patients in their local communities.

00:09:02.330 --> 00:09:09.330
Over the Easter period, looking out for April,
we have asked, 'we' being ourselves, Monitor,

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:16.020
the Trust Development Authority, that CCGs
continue to fund all of the extra services

00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:21.450
that were in place over the winter period
through April while the Better Care Fund kicks

00:09:21.450 --> 00:09:26.390
in and the contracting round is concluded.

00:09:26.390 --> 00:09:31.800
I think we cannot avoid the fact that there
clearly are very substantial cost pressures

00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:37.380
which have been building in the hospitals
over the course of this year. I think that

00:09:37.380 --> 00:09:43.140
has to be a call-to-action on the part of
the NHS collectively going into the new year.

00:09:43.140 --> 00:09:48.540
What I think it clearly shows is that the
single biggest cause of pressure has been

00:09:48.540 --> 00:09:54.720
the big increase in extra staffing, particularly
agency staffing, temporary staffing. What

00:09:54.720 --> 00:10:00.840
that shows is that the combination of a fair
national pay system but then leaving individual

00:10:00.840 --> 00:10:07.480
employers to try and hire temporary staff
by themselves, that combination is not working

00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:14.480
in that has produced an overspend of £697
million across just the Foundation Trust sector

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:16.710
in the first nine months of this year.

00:10:16.710 --> 00:10:21.070
That tells us something very important about
how we have got to change our approach to

00:10:21.070 --> 00:10:25.070
managing the workforce of the National Health
Service and it tells us something very important

00:10:25.070 --> 00:10:31.000
about how we are going to deliver on the broader
efficiencies, the £22 billion or so, that

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:36.250
are going to be required whoever turns out
to be the next government.

00:10:36.250 --> 00:10:41.540
This collective action issue is something
that NHS England together with our partners

00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:47.010
in the other five national leadership bodies
of the National Health Service and employers

00:10:47.010 --> 00:10:51.610
across the NHS are working on intensively
now. I suspect we are going to need to take

00:10:51.610 --> 00:10:58.610
a radically different view to things like
setting maximum rates for agency staff employees

00:10:59.240 --> 00:11:03.170
over the course of the next six months or
so. That is the sleeves rolled up conversation

00:11:03.170 --> 00:11:08.040
we have to have. It will be better for quality
of care. The RCN themselves say that there

00:11:08.040 --> 00:11:13.400
is enough funding to potentially convert that
spending into more than 20,000 permanent nurse

00:11:13.400 --> 00:11:18.300
posts. That is the job of work that we have
got to now engage with our partners across

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:21.500
the NHS on.

00:11:21.500 --> 00:11:27.970
Looking more broadly at the Five Year Forward
View agenda, we have made really quite substantial

00:11:27.970 --> 00:11:34.180
progress on kicking off a number of the active
ingredients that are going to be needed for

00:11:34.180 --> 00:11:41.180
success. We have got a paper later on in this
meeting, Malcolm, on the first 29 vanguards.

00:11:41.560 --> 00:11:48.560
The energy on the part of the many hundreds
of groups of nurses and doctors who came to

00:11:50.870 --> 00:11:54.810
talk at the Oval for three days, debate what
the future would look like, I think was a

00:11:54.810 --> 00:11:58.100
source of great encouragement and inspiration.

00:11:58.100 --> 00:12:03.260
We have with Public Health England kicked
off the first national diabetes prevention

00:12:03.260 --> 00:12:08.100
programme, which is obviously not going to
solve our health or financial problems next

00:12:08.100 --> 00:12:15.100
year but is an important start to a broader
programme of taking prevention seriously.

00:12:16.930 --> 00:12:22.770
We have got a discussion later on this morning
on health and social care. Again, with the

00:12:22.770 --> 00:12:25.830
Integrated Personal Commissioning bringing
together health and social care budgets for

00:12:25.830 --> 00:12:31.980
individuals and the, in principle, agreement
to move in that direction in Greater Manchester.

00:12:31.980 --> 00:12:37.730
I think NHS England is showing the seriousness
with which we are taking action on that agenda.

00:12:37.730 --> 00:12:44.140
Then today, in respect of the innovation agenda,
we are, together with the Office of Life Sciences

00:12:44.140 --> 00:12:49.270
and the Department of Health, kicking off
the worldwide call for innovators and entrepreneurs

00:12:49.270 --> 00:12:55.080
who want to come and use the NHS as a test
bed for improvement. We are never going to

00:12:55.080 --> 00:13:00.370
be the healthcare system that pays the highest
prices in the world but we ought to be the

00:13:00.370 --> 00:13:05.620
destination of choice for people wanting to
try new innovations that produce a good pay-off

00:13:05.620 --> 00:13:09.950
for patients and for tax-payers.

00:13:09.950 --> 00:13:16.030
This week we have also launched the Mental
Health Task force, which as prefigured in

00:13:16.030 --> 00:13:21.350
the Forward View, will help us chart the key
priorities for mental health improvement and

00:13:21.350 --> 00:13:26.470
parity of esteem over the next five years.
Similar to the process we have kicked off

00:13:26.470 --> 00:13:30.910
with cancer under the leadership of Harpal
Kumar.

00:13:30.910 --> 00:13:37.910
Today we are announcing the membership of
the Maternity Review Team that we, at the

00:13:38.010 --> 00:13:43.580
time of the Morecambe Bay inquiry, said would
be required in order to review the UK and

00:13:43.580 --> 00:13:49.450
the international evidence on responsive and
efficient maternity services, ensuring that

00:13:49.450 --> 00:13:53.940
women get safe and appropriate choices and
that staff are supported in the provision

00:13:53.940 --> 00:13:58.700
of that care.
We are asking Baroness Julia Cumberlege to

00:13:58.700 --> 00:14:05.700
chair that review panel with Sir Cyril Chantler
as the Vice-Chair and then a wide cross-section

00:14:06.560 --> 00:14:12.860
of interests and expertise including neighbourhood
midwives, consultant midwives, the National

00:14:12.860 --> 00:14:17.390
Childbirth Trust, Mumsnet, the Royal College
of Obstetricians &amp; Gynaecologists, the Royal

00:14:17.390 --> 00:14:24.390
College of Midwives. James Titcombe, who is
a bereaved father from Morecambe Bay, has

00:14:24.760 --> 00:14:30.700
also kindly agreed to serve as a member of
this panel. High expectations, important work

00:14:30.700 --> 00:14:35.930
to be done here on all of our behalf over
the course of the rest of the year.

00:14:35.930 --> 00:14:42.100
Finally, and we will talk about this in a
moment I know, Malcolm, our business plan

00:14:42.100 --> 00:14:47.300
for the year ahead: we expect, subject to
the Board approval today, to be able to publish

00:14:47.300 --> 00:14:48.370
that tomorrow.

00:14:50.200 --> 00:14:57.080
The last thing I should say by way of a piece
of important processes that work for the National

00:14:57.130 --> 00:15:04.130
Health Service is obviously we are about to
go into the election period and it is entirely

00:15:04.220 --> 00:15:09.940
legitimate and desirable that in a democracy
there should be vibrant debate on the future

00:15:09.950 --> 00:15:16.950
direction for the National Health Service.
All of us working in the NHS recall the standard

00:15:17.690 --> 00:15:23.520
guidance that applies to the National Health
Service over this period of time. Decisions

00:15:23.520 --> 00:15:30.520
on whether to permit visits to NHS property
are of course the responsibility of the individual

00:15:30.780 --> 00:15:36.220
NHS providers but there should not be disruption
to services and the same facilities should

00:15:36.220 --> 00:15:43.220
be made available to all candidates. Election
meetings should generally not be permitted

00:15:43.240 --> 00:15:44.830
on NHS premises.

00:15:44.830 --> 00:15:51.570
Those standing rules kick in the moment that
parliament is dissolved and the reality, of

00:15:51.570 --> 00:15:56.680
course, is that we go into this election with
the NHS having a plan for what the future

00:15:56.680 --> 00:16:03.680
needs to look like; a widely-agreed game plan
with a lot of agreement on the type of care

00:16:03.960 --> 00:16:09.970
re-design that is going to be required, a
clear proposition to whoever is the next government:

00:16:09.970 --> 00:16:11.800
'Give us the tools and we will do the job.'

00:16:11.800 --> 00:16:18.800
&gt;&gt; Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Simon, on that
high note. May I invite members of the Board

00:16:18.870 --> 00:16:25.870
to raise any queries or comments on the Chief
Executive's Report? No. Well done. Thank you

00:16:30.010 --> 00:16:34.070
for that and indeed much of what you have
presaged we are of course going to be coming

00:16:34.070 --> 00:16:37.910
to in the course of the meeting. Thank you
very much.

